WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.100
Botanists looked for the receptor of the plant hormone auxin at the cell surface for

2
00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:15.040
decades. Animal biology said a receptor catches the hormone, changes shape, and

3
00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:22.560
starts a cascade. But plants with a broken TIR1 gene respond poorly to auxin, and

4
00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:25.220
TIR1 is an F-box protein.

5
00:00:26.033 --> 00:00:31.413
An F-box protein is the interchangeable part of an SCF ligase.

6
00:00:32.373 --> 00:00:36.093
These proteins were thought to grab targets and sense nothing.

7
00:00:37.233 --> 00:00:43.053
Then, in the same issue of Nature, Estelle's laboratory at Indiana University and,

8
00:00:43.053 --> 00:00:48.113
independently, Ottoline Leyser and Stefan Kepinski at the University of York

9
00:00:48.113 --> 00:00:51.993
reported that TIR1 binds auxin itself.

10
00:00:52.967 --> 00:01:00.047
Auxin switches on genes, but repressor proteins of the Aux/IAA family hold them off.

11
00:01:00.787 --> 00:01:04.447
Without auxin, TIR1 binds one of them poorly.

12
00:01:05.587 --> 00:01:10.647
Auxin fits into a pocket at the exact place where the two proteins touch, and the

13
00:01:10.647 --> 00:01:16.407
grip tightens. The repressor is ubiquitinated and destroyed, and the genes come on.

14
00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:23.140
In 2007, Estelle's group, with Ning Zheng, solved the crystal structure.

15
00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:27.580
It showed auxin sitting in the joint between the two proteins.

16
00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:31.700
The hormone is part of the joint, a molecular glue.

17
00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:36.760
So detecting the hormone and carrying out its effect are a single event.
